Fix the aarch64-win64 epilogue for routines that need no frame pointer.

tcgaarch64.g_proc_exit emitted `mov sp,x29` whenever callee-saved registers
were stored, without checking pi_no_framepointer_needed. For a frameless
routine x29 still holds the caller's frame pointer, so the epilogue reset SP
to the caller's frame record and the caller then read and wrote its stack
slots at the wrong place. The observed symptom is an access violation at
startup (0xC0000005) in SysAllocateThreadVars on Windows 11 ARM64.

Upstream issue: https://gitlab.com/freepascal.org/fpc/source/-/issues/42091

Applied by .github/workflows/winarm64.yml to the pinned FPC_REF before the
cross compiler is built. Drop this patch when the pinned revision includes
the upstream fix.

--- a/compiler/aarch64/cgcpu.pas
+++ b/compiler/aarch64/cgcpu.pas
@@ -2236,8 +2236,11 @@
                 load_regs(list,R_INTREGISTER,RS_X19,RS_X28,R_SUBWHOLE);
                 { on Windows also restore SP even if the add should be enough
                   to have matching exit sequence to the entry sequence.
-                  Skip for exceptfilters where FP points to parent's frame, not local frame. }
+                  Skip for exceptfilters where FP points to parent's frame, not local frame,
+                  and for routines without a frame pointer: x29 still holds the caller's FP
+                  there, so mov sp,x29 would corrupt the stack (FPC #42091). }
                 if (target_info.system=system_aarch64_win64) and
+                   not (pi_no_framepointer_needed in current_procinfo.flags) and
                    (current_procinfo.procdef.proctypeoption<>potype_exceptfilter) then
                   a_load_reg_reg(list,OS_ADDR,OS_ADDR,NR_FP,NR_SP);
               end
